hi there Steve coffin here it's a  glorious day here in Vancouver's sunny  and warm it's the warmest day so far  this year normally I'd cover the  sunlight to get a better sort of  lighting for the video but it's just  such a glorious day I decided I would  leave the sunlight you know  flooding in here I want to talk about  making language-learning real and how  important that is to our success and I'm  gonna refer again to my most recent  experience with Romanian I had a goal I  had to go to Romania and that really  focused me on learning Romanian and I  think I learned quite a bit in the two  months that I had to prepare largely  listening and reading I was able after  the first three weeks love listening to  sort of more basic patterns and some of  the simple content we have a link I was  able to find radio Romania radio Romania  listen to news history and this kind of  thing plus and the second month I had  about 5 hours a week of discussion with  my tutors but I was still stumbling  it's amazing what basically I guess four  days I spoke a bit of Romanian in  Austria at a reception and then I was in  Romania for about four days and being in  the real environment it's it's not just  that you hear words many of them you've  heard before and you're speaking some  and I was speaking some with my tutors  but I've had this experience before that  when you're in the real environment your  language learning and take a real jump  forward there was a fellow on our farm  at link who was talking about going to  France and hoping that he would be able  to really improve his French while there  and I said in my experience he will  because you're now in a situation where  you're not talking to a tutor who speaks  to you slowly and who's understanding  and and whom you've paid you're talking  to people at the train station in the  shops who may or may not be patient may  or may not phrase  or pronounce things carefully and you've  got to respond to it and you're  surrounded by the language and the  newspapers on the radio and it's just a  different feeling although something the  language is not an academic exercise  it's real it's something you need to  communicate and it just changes the way  your your mind deals with the language  so much of language learning I believe  is it has to do with your emotions and  your attitudes toward the language do  you like the language do you not like  the language are you confident not  confident and of course this sense of  being in a real situation with the  language really can propel you forward  however there are some conditions I  firmly believe that if you are not  prepared if you go into a store and  someone barks something out you and you  haven't a chance to understand what  they're saying you know or I mean I was  again in Romania I had factory visit one  day and then we visited these  monasteries magnificent monasteries up  in the northern part of Moldova and then  the next day I had a car driver because  in Romania if you rent a car for another  20 euros a day you can get a driver so  we were six hours in the car six hours  we were speaking Romanian the whole way  now had I not been prepared I couldn't  possibly  I mean once I get past my name is how  old are you that's it if I don't have a  sufficient preparation so I firmly  believe that to take advantage of a  visit to a country and to get that  thrust forward in your in your language  skills prepare yourself ahead of time  and I've done this before I did it with  check-check took a lot longer than  Romanian because of of the greater  complexity and the fact that Romanian  has a lot of you know freebie words from  French and Italian and so forth but it's  the same idea you focus on the fact that  we're going to go somewhere and then you  prepare ahead of time then when you go  there you have this major breakthrough  because you're sufficiently well  prepared you know and I'm gonna ramble a  bit here but I was in a restaurant  locally here and one of our waitresses  had studied Mandarin for a year in a  high school here in Vancouver  and we with a Chinese couple and so we  said well can you say anything and so  she turned to the Chinese couple and all  she could say was washer Sarah nature  say in other words I'm Sarah who are you  first of all that's extremely polite you  don't speak to people like that you know  what's your name even in English you  know in the way seeing in Chinese but  that's all she had which is kind of sad  a year is a long time you can learn a  lot in a year now granted she's not very  motivated although her father's Chinese  her mother's you know not Chinese she  should have been motivated but  motivation is key that's why so many  people in the French language programs  in Canada don't learn because they're  not very motivated so how do you make it  you know how do you motivate them well  you you make it real because the  pleasure that I derived from my albeit  brief four days in Romania was immense I  mean I met this mayor of a small town in  Vienna his small town is where one of  these sawmills is located that we deal  with and he made me change my schedule  instead of going to brush off I ended up  going to his little town called my nest  and then we had a dinner with different  kinds of cheese and Mitch and and stuff  like that and he was such a nice guy  kissed me on both cheeks every time I  met him and then he insisted that I take  his car and driver a little little  Volkswagen is not like a limousine or  anything to go the next day to Sibiu  through the mountains which was like a  nine-hour Drive and he wouldn't say not  take no for an answer  and that's just one example of how  tremendously nice people were towards me  I was in Sibiu and the Sun was beating  down I didn't have a hat and I get  headaches from the Sun and I managed to  find this secondhand shop and they had a  little hat there but I mean we had a  lovely conversation and in Romania  people speak English well many people  speak English well she spoke English  well in fact this particularly was  married to someone from South America  but we had a great conversation and in  remaining and people were just so  friendly so you come away with this  wonderful experience like you've made  new friends it's  it's it's I'm unfortunately I shouldn't  say unfortunately but I have to go back  and concentrate on on Korea now because  I said I'm gonna learn that language but  it's it's it's always like this every  time you've had this brief fling with a  language Russian Czech Romanian I'm very  reluctant to leave it because I had so  many pleasant experiences associated  with the language but there are real  situations and that's why I think that  to prepare yourself for those real  situations I still think that intensive  listening and reading and building up  your vocabulary is the way to go you can  do it wherever you are you can do it on  your own you can follow your interests  if you're listening to and reading  things of interest it's meaningful more  meaningful than in a classroom where  they have role playing or what they call  task based language learning let's  pretend that you are buying groceries or  let's pretend that you are the checkout  girl and I find these things artificial  and not meaningful I far rather in my  interactions with my tutors just talk  about whatever we can come up with  whatever interests us that day  that's real therefore it's a real  meaningful what did you do today but she  asked me about my job I asked her we  talked about the politics we talked  about anything rather than trying to  artificially pretend that I walk in a  store if you have this broad base in the  language if you have a lot of words if  you've been listening the brain has  started to get used to hearing where  words begin and end picking up on  certain phrases if you go into the store  the first time somebody barks something  out - you don't get it but the next time  you 